WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.680
We’ve all got a card or handful of cards that make&nbsp;
us happy and somehow manage to wrangle their way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:09.680
into far more decks than one would expect just&nbsp;
because we want to play with them more. And this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:16.600
is a very normal, human impulse, an impulse that&nbsp;
I share. Skull Storm, Shigeki, Mask of Memory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:21.280
and the mages are cards that I always have lurking&nbsp;
in the back of my mind, begging me to put them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:27.800
into a deck. But, should that voice be listened&nbsp;
to? Should these pet cards be slammed in as many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:35.080
decks as possible? The answer to that is not a&nbsp;
clean yes or no, and depends on a couple factors.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:40.520
First of all, I should define here what I mean&nbsp;
by pet card. This is generally thought of as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:45.320
cards that players have some sort of fondness&nbsp;
toward, but pet cards can be pet cards for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:49.800
a variety of different reasons—players might&nbsp;
enjoy the art or flavor, they might find the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:54.360
card’s effect cool and unique, and they also&nbsp;
might just really enjoy what the card has to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.360 --> 00:01:00.320
offer for their deck. For starters, I want to&nbsp;
address the pet cards that are either generally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:03.880
bad, or so hyper-specific that it’s&nbsp;
difficult to find a deck where they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:09.560
truly feel at home. One good option for these&nbsp;
is to build a deck with the understanding that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:15.280
it’s here to fulfill the most primal of pet card&nbsp;
desires. If it’s a card you like for aesthetics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:19.400
build a deck that fits that aesthetic. If it’s&nbsp;
a card you like for its effect, find cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:24.200
that go with it in some way and make that the&nbsp;
broader thing. The deck likely won’t be great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:29.960
but that’s fine because it’s not here to be&nbsp;
great. I doubt any of this is controversial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:35.200
but I figure I should express it anyway since this&nbsp;
category of cards often does make up a nonzero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:40.440
portion of the cards that people consider their&nbsp;
pet cards. On the opposite end of the spectrum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:46.320
there are the pet cards that are just good, or at&nbsp;
least they’re flexible enough to never feel bad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:51.920
I poked around a bit in forums and saw a handful&nbsp;
of answers like Dawn Charm, Swan Song, and Kambal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:57.600
Consul of Allocation. And yeah, these cards are&nbsp;
just good, you can put them in a deck of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:03.160
colors and they will be almost guaranteed to feel&nbsp;
at least decent in a casual EDH game. I mentioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:08.320
Shigeki earlier, and I’d like to put that card in&nbsp;
this category as well—I run it in two different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:13.120
ramp-heavy green decks, and having a flexible&nbsp;
recursion ability that’s uncounterable and at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:18.560
instant is good enough that the card never feels&nbsp;
dead. Is that just cope on my part? Who knows!

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:24.360
With that, we’ve knocked out the cases of cards&nbsp;
that aren’t very good, and cards that are strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:30.080
and/or flexible enough to feel good in most games.&nbsp;
That leaves us with the stuff in the middle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:35.560
What does one do with a moderately potent&nbsp;
card with a somewhat specific use case? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:41.000
there are two options for this. Option A is you&nbsp;
take one or two pet cards, and build a specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:46.000
deck for them. Too often I’ve seen people keep&nbsp;
cards in their deck that are ill-fitting just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.760
because they like them. And while I’m not trying&nbsp;
to shame anybody for playing what they like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:55.200
I think it’s useful to keep in mind that you’re&nbsp;
not just doing your deck a disservice—you’re also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:00.800
doing that pet card a disservice. You can throw&nbsp;
these sorts of cards into any old deck whose color&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:06.560
identity they fit, but this doesn’t necessarily&nbsp;
mean they’ll feel satisfying. These sorts of cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:11.080
won’t reach their full potential if you’re playing&nbsp;
them in a deck they only tangentially connect to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:15.320
and at the worst you might gradually grow a&nbsp;
resentment for them if you keep drawing them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:20.720
rather than a card that is more useful. Meanwhile,&nbsp;
the act of building a deck that a pet card fully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:25.720
belongs in is the greatest act of love you can&nbsp;
show your favorite piece of cardboard, and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:30.760
can also be quite fun to build around a card&nbsp;
that you love. I’ve done this for Astral Slide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:35.800
Maze’s End, and Time Sieve, and in all three&nbsp;
cases it led to a deck that is a blast to play,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:40.280
and which I actively enjoy the process of&nbsp;
tuning. Some decks built this way will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:44.880
stronger than others due to the potential power&nbsp;
of the card in question—the Time Sieve deck is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:50.440
definitely the strongest of those last three—but&nbsp;
the principle still stands. Figuring out how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:55.760
best express a card, either in terms of power&nbsp;
level or in terms of vibe, is a lot of fun.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:59.840
The other option for utilizing niche,&nbsp;
moderately powerful pet cards is to put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:04.280
a bunch in one deck, basically to figure&nbsp;
out a deck that is well-equipped to handle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:08.840
a number of different types of effects. The&nbsp;
Astral Slide and Maze’s End decks I built,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:14.040
along with my Meria voltron deck, are all too&nbsp;
focused to throw other random pet cards into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:19.200
without incurring significant losses to deck&nbsp;
quality, but there are other types of deck that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:24.600
are much more flexible in what sorts of cards they&nbsp;
can accommodate. One of these is midrange decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:30.200
with simple goals and a solid core. My Radha deck&nbsp;
is running all sorts of fun cards that I enjoy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:35.120
and it’s able to do this because the types of&nbsp;
cards that the deck is looking for are 6-7 drops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:40.480
with some sort of meaningful game impact, and 4&nbsp;
cost cards that vaguely gesture at the concept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:45.520
of ramp. The deck is also built on a budget&nbsp;
and limited to being mid-power by its nature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:50.960
so it can accommodate a lot of cards that are&nbsp;
too weak for a higher power deck. My friend Hans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:55.600
has a Jensen Carthalion deck that’s basically&nbsp;
just five color beatdown, and the cards that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:59.840
make the cut for what the deck is looking for&nbsp;
is basically anything that lets it hit people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:05.760
with angel tokens more vigorously. If you want&nbsp;
a pithy rule of thumb, the more generic a deck’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:11.040
general game plan is, the more niche and&nbsp;
varied the specific card choices can be.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:15.480
In addition to generic midrange decks, there&nbsp;
are also specific themes that lend themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:20.120
to running more niche cards. One of these&nbsp;
is proliferate, a mechanic that I built at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:25.800
a low power level with a commander that I love&nbsp;
dearly—Skeleton ship. It contains lots of juicy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:30.720
cards I like such as Overwhelming Intellect,&nbsp;
Mind Unbound, a couple Parallax enchantments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:36.360
Grimoire of the Dead, and several questionable&nbsp;
storage lands, among others. None of these cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:41.200
are irredeemably bad, but they’re weird and niche&nbsp;
enough that I was struggling to find a home for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:46.680
them elsewhere. With the generic template of a&nbsp;
precon-power proliferate control deck, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:50.800
I can pack a ton of these cards and have them&nbsp;
all feel appropriate and all get their chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:56.080
to shine. This can also be scaled up in power&nbsp;
level—for a stronger proliferate deck you could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:00.880
run Atraxa, and with that there are all sorts&nbsp;
of options. Planeswalkers are one of the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:05.960
popular ones, but a friend of mine built Atraxa as&nbsp;
a ramp deck with a bunch of Myojins, which ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:11.760
up pretty entertaining. Some other themes that are&nbsp;
pretty flexible and often overlapping are tokens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:17.560
graveyard stuff, and synergies with a specific&nbsp;
card type such as artifacts or enchantments. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:21.400
key here is just that you need to figure out a&nbsp;
solid core to the deck—you should make sure it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:26.520
has a way to get off the ground, usually&nbsp;
by having enough lands, ramp, and draw,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:30.880
and then you can start to sprinkle in your spicier&nbsp;
stuff and add support where needed for different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:36.240
types of cards. It’s also good to make sure that&nbsp;
you don’t forget to run interaction—protection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:40.320
for vulnerable elements of the deck and removal to&nbsp;
make sure that opponents don’t run away with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:45.000
game are important aspects in most decks, and&nbsp;
they’re too often forgotten in the pursuit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:50.440
a fun vision. It may be tempting to disregard&nbsp;
these, but if you’re making a funny deck, how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:54.760
well-rounded it is may determine whether it ends&nbsp;
up being a deck you keep wanting to play with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:59.520
or whether it ends up getting set aside as&nbsp;
that gimmick deck that was fun for a while.
 &nbsp;

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:04.800
To reiterate a couple key points, pet cards are&nbsp;
a fundamental part of the social fabric in just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:09.280
about any collectible card game that exists,&nbsp;
and it’s obviously silly to suggestthat adding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:14.320
them to decks is flatly wrong. At the same time,&nbsp;
exercising a little bit of discretion in where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:19.800
these cards are played can lead to them being more&nbsp;
enjoyable. For those decent but somewhat niche pet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:25.000
cards out there, there are two options for fitting&nbsp;
them into decks: building a deck around a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:29.520
card, or building a deck that’s general enough&nbsp;
to fit a number of these specific cards in them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:34.960
A deck that’s already focused and gunning&nbsp;
to complete a specific task probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:39.920
isn’t a great spot for pet cards that are only&nbsp;
tangentially related to what the deck is doing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:45.760
but a more general deck can be a great place to&nbsp;
add in some funkier choices. As a final note,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:50.480
the fact that some cards don’t go well in some&nbsp;
decks is also why I’m such a big proponent of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:56.080
budget deckbuilding. I could throw $500 at a&nbsp;
fancy deck, but for that same amount I could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:00.760
make a half dozen decks at a lower budget point&nbsp;
that can all serve the interests of different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:05.400
gameplay styles and different cards I love.&nbsp;
When I’m building decks on a higher budget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:10.280
I need to think harder about how well a deck will&nbsp;
work and how much I’ll actually enjoy it in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:16.040
long term. But if I’m throwing together a $50 list&nbsp;
based on a card I really like or a funny premise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.040 --> 00:08:19.160
I can just order that sucker with no&nbsp;
second thoughts, knowing that if I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:25.120
get a half dozen fun games out of it I’ll&nbsp;
be happy. And to me, that’s pretty cool.

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:27.920
Thanks for watching! If you’ve got&nbsp;
some pet cards you really like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:32.160
you can let me know in the comments. Both because&nbsp;
I like hearing about fun cards people like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:44.960
and also because it serves my algorithmic&nbsp;
overlords. That’s a win-win if I ever heard one!

